course: dictionary
lesson: 5557
title: Lenders and borrowing
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A lender is a person or organisation that lends something to someone else, especially money. When you lend, you expect to get it back. The person who borrows has to pay it back.
explain: Banks and money companies are lenders too. «Some lenders charge huge interest, so £200 borrowed can grow to £400 owed.» Interest is extra money the borrower pays for using the lender's money. The lender is the one who gives the money out.
explain: Lending is not giving. «A gift usually becomes the receiver's for keeps», but «a borrowed book stays the lender's even if someone writes their name in it.» Lent things still belong to the lender.
explain: Lenders can make borrowing look easy with bright slogans like «Cash in minutes!» or «Buy now, pay later!». Before you agree, check how much interest you will have to pay back in total.
fact: A lender is a person or organisation that lends something, especially money.
fact: A borrower is the person who receives the loan and must pay it back.
fact: Interest is the extra money charged for borrowing, so the amount owed grows.
fact: A mortgage lender is a bank or company that lends money to buy a home.
fact: A loan shark is someone who lends money illegally at a huge cost.
fact: «Lending is not giving»: a lent book still belongs to the lender.
exercise: What is a lender? | A shop that sells loans to children | A person who only gives things away for free | *A person or organisation that lends something, especially money
exercise: «A borrowed book stays the lender's even if someone writes their name in it.» Who owns the book? | Nobody, once it has been borrowed | Whoever is reading it now | The person who wrote their name in it | *The lender
exercise: What is interest? | A prize for paying back early | The name of a bank | *Extra money paid on top of what was borrowed
test: «Cash in minutes!» is a slogan that makes borrowing look what? | Boring | *Easy | Impossible | Illegal
test: A mortgage lender is most like which of these? | A teacher who marks homework | A park where people play | *A bank that lends money to buy a house | A shop that sells sweets
exam: What is true about a book you have borrowed? | It belongs to whoever asks for it first | It belongs to the library shop | It becomes the borrower's for keeps | *It stays the lender's, even if your name is written in it
